-- Q1005: Advanced Basis Calculations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1366(d), S corporation losses are deductible only to the extent of the shareholder''s stock and debt basis. Losses first reduce stock basis to zero ($10,000 used), then the remaining $10,000 reduces debt basis from $15,000 to $5,000. The ordering is mandatory under Section 1367(a)(2).
Wrong (B): This reverses the required ordering. Stock basis must be reduced to zero before debt basis is touched, not the other way around.
Wrong (C): This fails to apply the remaining $10,000 of loss against debt basis. After stock basis reaches zero, the excess loss must reduce debt basis.
Wrong (D): This overstates the loss absorption. Only $20,000 of loss is allocated, which consumes $10,000 of stock basis and $10,000 of debt basis, leaving $5,000 of debt basis intact.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1005;

-- Q1006: Advanced Basis Calculations [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 1367(b)(2)(B) and Treas. Reg. Section 1.1367-2(c), when an S corporation shareholder''s losses have reduced both stock and debt basis, subsequent income restores debt basis first up to its original amount before increasing stock basis. This reversal of the loss absorption order ensures the shareholder''s loan is made whole before stock basis increases.
Wrong (B): This states the incorrect order. Stock basis is reduced first during losses, but income restores debt basis first — the sequences are reversed.
Wrong (C): There is no pro rata simultaneous restoration. The regulations require a specific ordering: debt basis first, then stock basis.
Wrong (D): Both stock and debt basis are restored when income is earned. The statute does not limit restoration to stock basis alone.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1006;

-- Q1007: Advanced Basis Calculations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 722 and Section 752, a partner''s initial outside basis equals cash contributed ($50,000) minus the decrease in individual liabilities assumed by the partnership ($30,000), plus the partner''s share of partnership liabilities ($30,000 x 40% = $12,000). The calculation is $50,000 - $30,000 + $12,000 = $32,000.
Wrong (A): This ignores the Section 752(b) deemed distribution from the partnership''s assumption of the partner''s $30,000 liability. The full $50,000 cash contribution alone does not determine basis.
Wrong (C): This incorrectly adds the full liability ($50,000 + $30,000 = $80,000) rather than netting the liability assumption against the partner''s share.
Wrong (D): This appears to calculate only the partner''s 40% share of the cash contribution, ignoring the actual basis computation rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1007;

