-- Q1019: Trusts and Estates [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 661(a), a trust''s distribution deduction equals the lesser of distributable net income (DNI) or the amount actually distributed to beneficiaries. DNI is $185,000 and actual distributions are $120,000, so the deduction is $120,000. The trust pays tax on the remaining $65,000 of undistributed income.
Wrong (A): The $200,000 figure is gross income before trustee fees. The distribution deduction cannot exceed DNI ($185,000) or actual distributions ($120,000), whichever is less.
Wrong (B): While DNI is $185,000, the trust only distributed $120,000. Section 661(a) caps the deduction at the lesser of DNI or amounts actually distributed.
Wrong (D): The $65,000 represents undistributed taxable income remaining after the deduction, not the deduction itself.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1019;

-- Q1020: Trusts and Estates [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 652(b) and Sec. 662(b), income distributed by a trust retains its character in the hands of the beneficiary. Since the trust''s DNI consists entirely of tax-exempt municipal bond interest under Sec. 103, the distribution carries out tax-exempt income. The beneficiary reports $0 of taxable income.
Wrong (A): This treats the distribution as fully taxable, ignoring the character-retention rule. Tax-exempt income does not become taxable merely because it passes through a trust.
Wrong (B): There is no rule that converts half of tax-exempt income to taxable income upon distribution. The full amount retains its exempt character.
Wrong (D): This is the same error as choice A. Municipal bond interest retains its tax-exempt character under Sec. 103 when distributed to a beneficiary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1020;

-- Q1021: Trusts and Estates [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 664(b) and Treas. Reg. Sec. 1.664-1(d)(1), charitable remainder trust distributions are characterized using a four-tier ordering system: (1) ordinary income, (2) capital gains, (3) other tax-exempt income, and (4) corpus. The $60,000 exhausts all three income tiers ($40,000 + $15,000 + $5,000) before reaching corpus.
Wrong (A): This treats the entire $60,000 as ordinary income, ignoring the four-tier ordering. Capital gains and tax-exempt income must be allocated before characterizing everything as ordinary.
Wrong (C): This skips the capital gains tier and jumps to corpus. The four-tier system requires exhausting capital gains ($15,000) and tax-exempt income ($5,000) before any return of corpus.
Wrong (D): This treats the entire distribution as capital gain, which violates the worst-in-first-out ordering. Ordinary income in Tier 1 must be distributed first.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1021;

